# KSV-0039 (LimitRange) and KSV-0040 (ResourceQuota) fire per-file, not
# per-namespace: `trivy fs` scans each YAML file in isolation with no
# visibility into sibling files, so it flags every namespaced resource in
# every file that doesn't *also* contain its own ResourceQuota+LimitRange
# pair - including policy/21-resource-quotas.yaml and
# policy/20-limitrange-default.yaml themselves, and dozens of files that
# create no pods at all (Argo CD Applications, ClusterIssuers, CRD installs).
# Confirmed reproducible locally: 266 findings across ~40 files with these
# two checks alone, dropping to 0 with this ignore and nothing else changed
# (verified diff was exactly 266 misconfig findings, before vs after).
#
# The actual guardrail is real and live - see policy/21-resource-quotas.yaml
# and policy/20-limitrange-default.yaml, applied to every namespace in
# policy/10-default-deny.yaml except kube-system (deliberately excluded,
# see that file's own header comment) - verified against the live cluster
# with `kubectl get resourcequota,limitrange -A`. This ignore just stops a
# scanner that structurally cannot see that fix from re-flagging it forever.
KSV-0039
KSV-0040
